    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, can u explain how a capacitor behaves differently in a dc circuit vs an ac circuit? also whats the formula for capacitive reactance? i need to know this for my exam tomorrow.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely! In a DC circuit, a capacitor blocks current once it's fully charged, essentially acting as an open circuit after a brief initial charging period. In an AC circuit, the capacitor continuously charges and discharges as the current changes direction, allowing current to pass through in parallel with the circuit. The formula for capacitive reactance (Xc) is Xc = 1/(2πfC), where f is the frequency (in Hz) and C is the capacitance (in Farads). Hope that helps! Good luck with your exam!",
